{"qid":"Q1041","names":["Republic of Senegal"],"title":"Republic of Senegal","endonym":{"native":"Senegaal","lang":"wo"},"capsule":"The Republic of Senegal achieved independence from France on 20 June 1960, initially as part of the short-lived Mali Federation before becoming a fully sovereign republic on 20 August 1960 under President Léopold Sédar Senghor, one of the foremost African intellectuals of the twentieth century. Senegal's multiparty democracy, though punctuated by political crises, survived peaceful transfers of power under Senghor (who retired in 1981), Abdou Diouf, and Abdoulaye Wade, making it one of West Africa's most stable states. The country's economy rests on groundnut exports, phosphate mining, fishing, and remittances, while Dakar — built on the Cap-Vert peninsula — functions as a major regional hub for commerce and diplomacy. A separatist insurgency in the southern Casamance region has persisted since 1982 without resolution.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Modern state boundaries are precisely defined; territorial extent is not in dispute.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Senegal","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Senegal"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Senegal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senegal"}],"britannica":"place/Senegal","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Senegal","rulers":[{"qid":"Q739956","name":"Mamadou Dia","start":1957,"end":1962,"image":"Mamadou Dia 1962.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mamadou%20Dia%201962.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","artist":"Bruno Christophe","attributionRequired":true